WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.100
Every autumn, an oak drops its leaves onto the forest floor.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.380
New leaves land on the old ones, year after year.

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.460
So why isn't the forest floor buried?

4
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.730
Think of the leaf pile as a bathtub.

5
00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:21.450
Each autumn, a new layer of leaves pours in.

6
00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.890
But the pile stays low, year after year.

7
00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.530
Something must be draining it.

8
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:29.590
Start with the tap.

9
00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:31.950
The oak is a producer.

10
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.530
It captures energy from sunlight and stores it in sugar.

11
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.510
It builds its leaves from carbon dioxide water and nutrients from the soil.

12
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.040
A deer eats acorns.

13
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.320
It eats a producer, so it is a first-order consumer.

14
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.400
A mouse eats seeds, and a fox eats the mouse.

15
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.900
That makes the fox a second-order consumer.

16
00:00:57.983 --> 00:01:01.883
Consumers carry some matter along from the tap toward the drain.

17
00:01:03.083 --> 00:01:05.863
But sooner or later, every animal dies too.

18
00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:09.443
Its body joins the dead leaves in the pile.

19
00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:12.197
Now look down into the pile.

20
00:01:13.637 --> 00:01:15.557
Fungus threads spread through the dead leaves.

21
00:01:16.937 --> 00:01:19.757
Bacteria, far too small to see, work beside them.

22
00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:22.237
These are decomposers.

23
00:01:23.497 --> 00:01:26.557
They break down dead plants, dead animals and wastes.

24
00:01:27.757 --> 00:01:29.117
A fungus has no mouth.

25
00:01:30.337 --> 00:01:33.917
It releases chemicals that break the leaf down outside its body.

26
00:01:35.177 --> 00:01:36.637
Then it takes in the small pieces.

27
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:44.120
Here is a puzzle. In one forest study, fallen leaves lost close to half their mass

28
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:49.160
in a year. When decomposers use a leaf, where does much of it end up?

29
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.880
Into the soil, or into the air?

30
00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:00.153
Much of it goes into the air.

31
00:02:01.613 --> 00:02:03.333
Decomposers use the leaf as food.

32
00:02:04.613 --> 00:02:07.333
Like you, they release its energy by respiration.

33
00:02:08.513 --> 00:02:10.833
That gives off carbon dioxide and water.

34
00:02:12.153 --> 00:02:15.713
Some matter stays in their bodies, and rain washes some into the soil.

35
00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:18.687
A smaller part matters a lot.

36
00:02:20.107 --> 00:02:22.647
Nutrients, such as nitrogen, go into the soil.

37
00:02:23.987 --> 00:02:26.127
The oak's roots take them up and build new leaves.

38
00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:29.267
The matter has gone all the way around.

39
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.130
Energy does something different.

40
00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:34.550
It comes in as sunlight.

41
00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.470
Every living thing here gives some of it off as heat.

42
00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.170
Energy does not go around.

43
00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.450
Now watch the pile in a simple model.

44
00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.450
The same leaf fall comes each autumn.

45
00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.790
Decomposers drain the same share of the pile each year.

46
00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.530
The pile grows until the drain takes out as much as falls in.

47
00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:58.790
Then it stops growing.

48
00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:03.670
A real forest floor levels off only when it reaches that balance.

49
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.290
Producers fill the pile.

50
00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.190
Consumers carry matter along.

51
00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:14.210
Decomposers drain the leaf pile, so the matter goes around again.
